# gzip — real-world examples

## Compress a file (replaces it with .gz)

```sh
gzip test.txt
```

## Compress to a specific location via stdout

```sh
gzip -c test.txt > test_custom.txt.gz
```

## Uncompress a .gz file

```sh
gzip -d test.txt.gz    # or: gunzip test.txt.gz
```

## Show compression ratios

```sh
gzip -l *.gz
```

## Recursively compress everything under a directory

```sh
gzip -r documents_directory
```

## Compress but keep the original

```sh
gzip -k test.txt            # -k keeps the source file
gzip < test.txt > test.txt.gz   # redirect trick, same effect
```

## Read a compressed log without extracting it

```sh
zcat /var/log/zypper.log-20260701.gz | less
zgrep -i error /var/log/messages-*.gz
```

## Max compression for a backup shipped off the homelab

```sh
tar cf - /srv/plex/config | gzip -9 > plex-config-$(date +%F).tar.gz
```

| Flag | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| -d | decompress |
| -c | write to stdout, keep original |
| -k | keep original file |
| -l | list compression stats |
| -r | recurse directories |
| -1 .. -9 | speed vs. compression (default -6) |
| -v | verbose |
